I"&<p>This paper hypothesizes that private firm financial reporting is of lower quality comparing to public firms. In this study, financial reporting quality is conceptually framed as timely economic losses recognition, and is empirically measured by Basu’s (1997) time-series measure of timely loss recognition and a accruals-based method (Dechow 1994, Dechow et al. 1998).  As is explained in the following quote (Ball and Shivakumar, 2005, pp. 84):</p>

<p><i>We interpret reporting quality in abstract terms as the usefulness of financial statements to investors, creditors, managers and all other parties contracting with the firm. Following Basu (1997), we measure a single but nevertheless important attribute of reporting quality: timeliness in financial statement recognition of economic losses. Timely loss recognition increases financial statement usefulness generally, particularly in corporate governance and debt agreements.</i></p>

<p>This paper finds evidence that private firms 1) report less reversal of income decreases than public companies and 2) have less correlated accruals and operating cashflows, both reflecting a lower frequency of timely loss recognition due to lower demand for financial reporting quality. However, the authors emphasize that this study should not be interpreted as a supporting evidence of a stricter regulation of financial reporting for private firms. In contrast, “<i>lower earnings quality in private firms is an optimal outcome in the market for financial reporting, not a failure in supply</i>”.</p>

<h5>Theory</h5>
<p>The theoretical section related to accounting conservatism is as good as a brief but thorough literature review. I note down two important questions that the authors address in this paper.</p>

<p><b>Why timeliness of economic loss incorporation is an important attribute of earnings quality?</b></p>

<p>Because it makes financial statements more useful in several contexts, for example in corporate governance and loan agreements. First, managers will avoid investing in negative NPV projects if they know ex-ante that any economic losses incurred will be recognized in a timely manner. In this sense, timely loss recognition constrains managerial opportunistic behavior and therefore improves corporate governance. Second, timely loss recognition makes it easier to trigger debt covenants and provides a lower bind of asset value, which facilitates transfer of control when firms enter into financial distress.</p>

<p><b>What and why are there two types of conservatism, which are known as conditional and unconditional conservatism?</b></p>

<p>Watts and Zimmerman (1986) provides a definition for unconditional conservatism:</p>

<p><i>Conservatism means that the accountant should report the lowest value among the possible alternative values for assets and the highest alternative value for liabilities. Revenues should be recognized later rather than sooner and expenses sooner than later.</i></p>

<p>Basu (1997) contributes to the definition of conditional conservatism:</p>

<p><i>I interpret conservatism as capturing accountants’ tendency to require a higher degree of verification for recognizing good news than bad news in financial statements. Under my interpretation of conservatism, earnings reflects bad news more quickly than good news. </i></p>

<p>Their main difference in definition, in summary, is that conditional conservatism is <b>news-dependent</b> but unconditional conservatism is not. The authors also comment on the contracting value of these two forms of conservatism: “<i>In a contracting setting unconditional conservatism seems at best neutral (if the bias is known) and possibly inefficient (if the bias is unknown). In contrast, conditional conservatism involves timely loss recognition, and thereby increases the efficiency of debt and compensation/governance contracting. From a contracting perspective, conditional and unconditional conservatism are substantially different concepts.</i>”</p>

<p>As about the relationship/interaction between these two forms of conservatism, Beaver and Ryan (2005) provides an insightful analysis.</p>

<p>Regarding the hypothesis development of information channels in private and public firms, the authors argue that private firms supply less timely recognition for economic losses in response to less demand from the users of financial reports, because private firms are more likely to communicate privately with shareholders and stakeholders, thereby reducing the demand for public financial reporting quality.</p>

<h5>Empirics</h5>
<p>This paper presents two models to assess the timeliness of loss recognition.</p>

<p>&amp;Delta NI_t = alpha_0</p>

<p>The first model measures the persistence of net income. In theory, if firms report gains in a more timely manner than losses, then gains should be more persistent than losses, and do not tend to reverse. Therefore, alpha1 is expected to be 0. And because losses are transitory and tend to reverse in the following period, alpha2+alpha3 is expected to be negative. The incremental persistence, which is measured by alpha3, should be negative.
The second model is adapted from Dechow et al. (1998) and is focused on two functionalities of accruals: 1) mitigating noise in operating cash flow; 2) timely recognition of economic gains and losses. The first role implies that beta2 should be negative (Dechow et al. 1998), the second role implies that beta 3 should be positive. The rationale behind the second role of accruals is that there should be a positive but asymmetric correlation between accruals and contemporaneous cash flows, because revisions in current period cash flow are positively correlated with current revisions in expected future cash flows. For example, an investment experiencing decreased current period cash flow is likely also to be experiencing a downward revision in its future prospects: i.e., in its expected future cash flows.</p>

<h5>Note</h5>
<p>This paper develops a new model related to accruals to measure timeliness of economic loss recognition, which can be applied to future studies. 
It could add some sort of test to examine whether the reporting quality gap between private and public firms results from demand or supply side. Although the authors have made an argument on this point but the mechanisms are not formally tested.</p>

